\section{Response on the formula left-hand side}{

The cover response is a single length-N vector, so it may sit on the top
formula left-hand side and \verb{y =} is dropped. The two
calls are equivalent:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# response on the LHS (y = omitted)
tobs(cover.flat ~ time.sc + habitat +
       spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj),
     data = dat, family = cover(response = "beta"),
     method = "nested_laplace")

# the same fit with a one-sided formula and an explicit y =
tobs(~ time.sc + habitat +
       spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj),
     y = dat$cover.flat, data = dat, family = cover(response = "beta"),
     method = "nested_laplace")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Naming the response makes the per-arm spatial labels read naturally: \code{cover()}
splits \code{cover.flat} into a \code{presence} arm and a \code{positive} arm, the arm names
that per-arm formulas and share() address. The LHS is evaluated against \code{data}
(then the calling environment), so it may be a bare column or an expression.
}

\section{Joint nested-Laplace engine — spatial-prior parameterisation}{

When fitted with \code{method = "nested_laplace"} and an areal spatial term in
the latent-presence formula (\code{bym2(graph = adj)}, or \code{car()} /
\code{car_proper()}), the engine identifies a single latent field \code{z} per region
and parameterises the two arms as \verb{eta_occ = X beta_occ + sigma * z}
and \verb{eta_pos = X beta_pos + alpha * sigma * z}. Both BYM2 sub-blocks
(\code{phi}, \code{theta}) are subject to hard sum-to-zero constraints (see
\code{.joint_inner_var()} for the math): the cover-arm intercept
\code{beta_pos[1]} is identified as the population mean of \code{eta_pos}
under \code{mean(z) = 0}. Any simulator generating data for this engine
must demean both \code{phi_f} and \code{theta_f} before scaling, otherwise the
estimator targets \code{beta_pos_0_truth + alpha * mean(w_s_sim)} and
coverage of the \emph{population} truth collapses with alpha. See
\code{\link[=simulate_cover_joint]{simulate_cover_joint()}} for a ready-made demeaned simulator.
}

\section{Spatially varying trend (a weighted areal term)}{

A spatially varying trend is model structure, so it is declared in the
formula as a SECOND, weighted areal term on the same graph as the intercept
field, not through \code{control}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{~ time.sc +
  icar(graph = adj, group_var = "cell_idx") +
  icar(graph = adj, weight = time.sc, group_var = "cell_idx")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The unweighted term is the shared intercept field; the weighted term
\code{icar(..., weight = col)} is the trend field, whose contribution to each
arm's predictor is \code{weight_i * z[cell_i]}, coupled onto the cover arm with
its own scale (\code{alpha_trend}, reported in \code{fit$alpha_trend} /
\code{fit$sigma_trend}) integrated over the outer grid. The umbrella spelling
\code{spatial(graph = adj, model = "icar", weight = col)} resolves identically.
The trend coupling grid defaults to \code{control$alpha.grid}; override it with
\code{control$alpha.grid.trend}. Requires \code{method = "nested_laplace"}. A coupled
trend cannot currently combine with \code{temporal()} / \code{re()} blocks in the same
fit.

The axis the amplitude rides carries prior structure -- a point mass at
\code{alpha = 0} ("no coupling") and a log-spaced slab above it -- so it is set
in one of two ways. \code{share(alpha = grid(...))} STATES its nodes, and with them
that structure. \code{share(alpha = grid(n = 9))} states a RESOLUTION: the engine
re-reads its own axis with that many slab nodes, point mass and slab bounds
unchanged, so sharpening the axis never restates its structure. \verb{terms =}
gives either, per block. The resolution is the only way to raise this axis,
because it does not densify when the donor \code{control$sigma.grid} does: on an
informative data set the outer grid's quadrature effective sample size
saturates on the copy amplitude while every other axis tracks the request
(measured engine-side, \code{NOTES_measurements.md}). One block takes one of the
two; giving both is an error. \code{control$alpha.grid[.trend]} and
\code{control$alpha.n[.trend]} are the lower-level spelling of the same pair.

Set it when the fit's hyperparameter intervals are reported. On the coupled
SBC fixture the declared resolution leaves the field SD miscalibrated once
the data are informative -- at 10 visits per site its uniformity p-value is
9.1e-05, against 0.17 at 3 visits -- and \code{control$alpha.n = 21} returns every
scored quantity to nominal at both. Thirteen slab nodes do not (the field SD
still reads 4.5e-03), and the price is a 2.3-3x longer fit, since the outer
grid is a tensor. Point estimates are not affected the way the intervals are;
the measurement is in \code{NOTES_measurements.md}.
}

\section{Varying-coefficient spatial bar (the compact single-term form)}{

The intercept field plus its weighted trend field can also be written as one
\code{spatial()} term carrying an lme4-style coefficient formula:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{~ time.sc + habitat +
  spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The bar left-hand side spells the coefficient fields: the intercept column
(\code{1}) is the unweighted field; each covariate column (\code{time.sc}) is a
weight-scaled coefficient field (\code{weight_i * z[cell_i]}). The bar right-hand
side (\code{cell_idx}) is the graph node index (the areal \code{group_var}); \code{||}
requests independent intercept and slope fields, a single \code{|} makes them
correlated. This desugars to exactly the two-term weighted-areal form above,
so the two spellings give the same fit.
}

\section{Choosing a field's arm}{
 placement and share():
The cover hurdle's two arms are \code{presence} (the \code{y > 0} Bernoulli arm) and
\code{positive} (the \code{y | y > 0} arm); \code{summary()} and the coefficient output print
these same labels. A field is placed on an arm by writing it in that arm's
per-arm formula (\code{presence = ~ ...}, \code{positive = ~ ...}), and shared across
arms with share(). A field in the single shared \code{formula} reaches both arms.

\emph{Both arms (shared / copied).} A field in the shared \code{formula} is one
presence-anchored latent copied onto the positive arm with an estimated
coupling per coefficient field (\code{alpha} for the intercept field, \code{alpha_trend}
for the trend field), marginalized on the outer grid:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# one latent field, shared by both arms
tobs(cover.flat ~ time.sc + habitat +
       spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj),
     data = dat, family = cover(response = "beta"), method = "nested_laplace")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{presence: eta_presence = ... + u_cell + time.sc * s_cell
positive: eta_positive = ... + alpha * u_cell + alpha_trend * time.sc * s_cell
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Per-arm formulas make the shared field explicit: place it on \code{presence} and
copy it onto \code{positive}. \code{share(spatial())} estimates the coupling on the
default axis; \code{share(spatial(), alpha = grid(c(...)))} integrates it over
supplied nodes, \code{alpha = grid(n = 9)} over the engine's own axis read at \code{n}
slab nodes, and \code{alpha = 0.5} fixes it. \code{share(spatial(), prior = list(...))}
regularizes the coefficient itself; a fit copying both the intercept and a
weighted trend block is refused it, since one prior reaches the engine per
fit (gcol33/tulpa#655).

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{tobs(presence = ~ time.sc + habitat +
                  spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj),
     positive = ~ time.sc + habitat + share(spatial()),
     data = dat, family = cover(response = "beta"), method = "nested_laplace")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\emph{One arm only (free / separate).} A field written in one arm's formula is
a separate latent on that arm alone, with its own precision and no cross-arm
coupling. A field in each arm's formula gives two
independent latents:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{tobs(presence = ~ time.sc + spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj),
     positive = ~ time.sc + spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj),
     data = dat, family = cover(response = "beta"), method = "nested_laplace")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{presence: eta_presence = ... + u_presence_cell + time.sc * s_presence_cell
positive: eta_positive = ... + u_positive_cell + time.sc * s_positive_cell
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The free fit reports \code{sigma_armspecific}. Arm-specific fields are their own
spatial structure: they do not combine with a shared field, a weighted trend,
or \code{temporal()} / \code{re()} in the same formula, and at most one targets each arm.

The \code{||} and \code{|} axis is separate from shared / free: \code{||} makes the intercept
and slope fields independent, while a single \code{|} makes them correlated (a free
cross-covariance, MCAR). A correlated \code{|} bar shared across both arms (in the
shared \code{formula}, or on \code{presence} with \code{share()} on \code{positive}) is copied with
one estimated amplitude; placed on one arm's formula it is a free-Sigma
correlated field on that arm alone, no cross-arm copy.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{field in the shared formula, no share()   one presence-arm latent, pinned at alpha = 0
field + share() (either spelling)         one shared / copied latent (presence anchor, coupling estimated)
a field in each arm's formula             separate / free latents, no coupling
||                                        independent intercept and slope coefficient fields
|                                         correlated (MCAR) coefficient fields, copy-only
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Without a \code{share()} (and without \code{control$alpha.grid}) the presence field is
NOT carried onto the cover arm: the amplitude is pinned at \code{alpha = 0} and the
field rides the presence arm alone. Structure nobody wrote is not in the
model, the reading \code{occu_cover()} and \code{occu_multiscale_cover()} also have.
Write \code{+ share(spatial())} in the shared formula, or place the field on
\code{presence} and \code{share(spatial())} on \code{positive}, to couple the two arms. A
correlated \code{|} bar is the exception: that spelling is copy-only by
definition, so the bar itself states the coupling.
}

\section{A formula bar is a random effect, not a spatial field}{

A bare lme4 bar in the formula -- \code{(1 | cell)}, \code{(1 + x | cell)},
\code{(x || cell)} -- is a grouped random effect, not a spatial field, even when the
grouping factor names the areal graph nodes. The engine's inline-MCAR call
\code{tulpa::spatial(graph, ~ 1 + x | cell)} reads \verb{| cell} as a separable spatial
field, but the same spelling inside a \code{cover()} formula is parsed as an IID
random effect on \code{cell}. For a spatial field on the cells write either the
compact \code{spatial()} bar of the section above,
\code{spatial(~ 1 + x || cell, graph = adj)}, or the two-term weighted-areal form,
\verb{icar(graph = adj, group_var = "cell") +}
\code{icar(graph = adj, weight = x, group_var = "cell")}. When a formula carries a
bar whose grouping factor is also an areal term's \code{group_var}, \code{cover()} emits
a one-time message noting the bar is fitted as a random effect; suppress it
with \code{\link[base:suppressMessages]{base::suppressMessages()}}.
}

\section{Checkpoint / resume}{

A spatial cover-hurdle fit integrates over a large outer hyperparameter grid
and can run for hours. \code{control$checkpoint = list(path = "fit.ckpt", resume = TRUE)} makes the joint engine append each completed grid cell to \code{path}; a
\code{resume = TRUE} run loads the finished cells and solves only the rest,
reproducing the from-scratch fit, so a killed or rebooted fit resumes instead
of restarting. \code{resume = FALSE} starts a fresh file. Forwarded to
\code{\link[tulpa:tulpa_nested_laplace_joint]{tulpa::tulpa_nested_laplace_joint()}}.
}

